% ============================================================================
% weather-instruments — each common weather-observing instrument and the ONE
% quantity it measures (adj-facts-stdlib, METEOROLOGY).
% ============================================================================
% A genuinely new axis in the "observation and measurement" gap ADJ-STDLIB-
% COVERAGE.md 5.1 names as a Major Gap for K-8 science — a DIFFERENT
% measurement domain from the already-shipped `chemistry/measuring-tools.adj`
% (lab tools: ruler/graduated cylinder/balance/thermometer). This table maps
% a WEATHER-OBSERVING instrument to the quantity it measures:
% `weather_instrument(instrument, quantity)`. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? weather_instrument(barometer, $Q)   % atmospheric_pressure
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `weather_instrument(instrument, quantity)` carrying the NOAA citation, so
% the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns
% the quantity WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on an instrument not one of the six the cited page names. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a quantity and recall the instrument that
% measures it:
%
%     ? weather_instrument($I, humidity)   % hygrometer
%
% The six instruments and the quantity each one measures, as a little truth
% table:
%
%     instrument      quantity                the cited heading
%     -------------   ---------------------   -----------------------------------
%     anemometer      wind_speed              "Build an Anemometer to Measure Wind Speed"
%     weather_vane    wind_direction          "Build a Weather Vane to Find Wind Direction"
%     barometer       atmospheric_pressure    "Build a Barometer for Measuring Atmospheric Pressure"
%     thermometer     air_temperature         "Build a Screened Thermometer to Measure Air Temperature"
%     hygrometer      humidity                "Build a Hygrometer to Measure Humidity"
%     rain_gauge      rainfall                "Build a Rain Gauge to Measure Rainfall"
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from NOAA's "Build Your Own Weather Station"
% education page, whose six section headings each name one instrument and
% the quantity it measures (or, for the weather vane, "finds"). WebFetch-
% verified TWICE for consistency before writing. `trust authoritative` —
% oceanservice.noaa.gov is a primary U.S. government (NOAA) science-
% education source, the same tier `precipitation-types.adj`'s and
% `wind-scale.adj`'s NOAA/NWS sources earned. An instrument not one of
% these six — a seismometer, a compass, a microscope — has no row, so a
% recall on it ABSTAINS rather than inventing a quantity. (See
% ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table weather_instrument {
    columns instrument, quantity

    row (anemometer, wind_speed)
    row (weather_vane, wind_direction)
    row (barometer, atmospheric_pressure)
    row (thermometer, air_temperature)
    row (hygrometer, humidity)
    row (rain_gauge, rainfall)

    source "Build an Anemometer to Measure Wind Speed"
    locator "https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/education/dyw-weather-station.html"
    trust authoritative
}
